    "answer_to_60k_question": "Across every session measured here a single controller instance on this hardware absorbed between 34 and 114 packet_in events per second end to end, including classification and rule installation. The spread is a property of the session configuration, not of the offered rate: the sweep sessions run 45 s with no background traffic and the fixed-load sessions run 150 s with it, and the sweep is saturated at every offered rate tested, so it bounds goodput rather than locating the knee. The 60,606 flows/s figure in the submitted manuscript was an offline feature-and-inference rate measured without any control channel, and it is not an ingest rate; it is two to three orders of magnitude above anything measured here. Reaching that order of magnitude at the control plane requires the flows not to arrive as individual table-miss events: switch-side aggregation, packet_in rate limiting and sampled export are the mechanisms, and their cost is quantified in the flow-table analysis of E8b.",
